Q: Is it possible to serialize a kotlin enum class without a custom serializer? A: <p>Gson serializes enum constants as plain strings using the constant name, like <code>&quot;A001&quot;</code>. It won't pick up the properties on your <code>ImageCodes</code> class automatically, and there's no way to configure that without writing a custom <code>TypeAdapter</code>.</p>
<p>But if you switch to Jackson, you can get object serialization with one annotation:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonFormat

@JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.OBJECT)
internal enum class ImageCodes(
    val dateRequired: Boolean,
    val category: DocumentCategory,
    val excludedFor: List&lt;IdocsDocumentOwnerCategory&gt; = listOf()
) {
    A001(true, DocumentCategory.TAX_RETURN, listOf(IdocsDocumentOwnerCategory.Student)),
    // ...
}

val mapper = ObjectMapper()
val json = mapper.writeValueAsString(ImageCodes.A001)
// result: {&quot;name&quot;:&quot;A001&quot;,&quot;dateRequired&quot;:true,&quot;category&quot;:&quot;TAX_RETURN&quot;,&quot;excludedFor&quot;:[&quot;Student&quot;]}
</code></pre>
<p>I'd just iterate over the entries to build your config file. Deserializing this back into the enum would require a <code>@JsonCreator</code>, but for generating the JSON itself, this is enough.</p>

Q: Select smallest and biggest value of an object A: <p>The code of Sam should look like this:</p>

<pre><code>$lowest_value = null;
$highest_value = null;
foreach($data as $obj) { //$data should be the array containing the result objects.
    $start = $obj-&gt;start;
    $end = $obj-&gt;end;

    if ($lowest_value === null)
      $lowest_value = $start;
    else
      if ($lowest_value &gt; $start)
        $lowest_value = $start;

    if ($highest_value === null)
      $highest_value = $end;
    else
      if ($highest_value &lt; $end)
        $highest_value = $end;
 } 
</code></pre>

<p>can be shortened to : </p>

<pre><code>    if ($lowest_value === null || $lowest_value &gt; $start)
      $lowest_value = $start;

    if ($highest_value === null || $highest_value &lt; $end)
      $highest_value = $end;
</code></pre>

<p>Both values can be generated within one iteration without problems. Keep in mind that the <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> works in the case you keep the date formated as given. Otherwhise you would need to use date-functionality to compare the dates.</p>

<p>You could also use MySQL to query directly for the Min/Max values using the Aggregation methods Min or Max: <a href="http://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/5.0/en/group-by-functions.html" rel="nofollow">http://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/5.0/en/group-by-functions.html</a></p>

<p>(If you don't need each object to be loaded this will be faster)</p>

Q: Is there a difference between setting FileSystemInfo.LastWriteTime and using File.SetLastWriteTime? A: <p>The main difference is that <code>FileInfo.LastWriteTime</code> updates the object's internal cache, while <code>File.SetLastWriteTime</code> doesn't.</p>
<p>If you set the property on a <code>FileInfo</code> instance, the getter returns that value immediately without a new disk hit. But if you call <code>File.SetLastWriteTime</code> on the path, any existing <code>FileInfo</code> objects will keep holding the old timestamp until you call <code>Refresh()</code>.</p>
<p>So <code>File.SetLastWriteTime</code> is usually better for one-off updates because it avoids the overhead of creating an object. I'd only use <code>FileInfo</code> if I'm already working with an instance or need to read the value back immediately.</p>
<p>Try this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var info = new FileInfo(@&quot;C:\example.txt&quot;);

// Setting via FileInfo updates its cache
info.LastWriteTime = new DateTime(2020, 1, 1);
Console.WriteLine(info.LastWriteTime); // Shows 2020-01-01 immediately

// Setting via File bypasses the cache
File.SetLastWriteTime(@&quot;C:\example.txt&quot;, new DateTime(2021, 1, 1));
Console.WriteLine(info.LastWriteTime); // STILL shows 2020-01-01 (stale cache)
info.Refresh();
Console.WriteLine(info.LastWriteTime); // Now shows 2021-01-01
</code></pre>

Q: How to initialize struct members in struct in constructor new A: <p>Structs are initialized by their fields, not fields of any nested structs. You should make those first:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>let i_cache = CacheInformation {
    size: i_cache_size,
    lines: i_cache_lines,
    ...
};

return Cache {
    i_cache: i_cache,
    ...
};
</code></pre>
<p>(There's a shorthand for when field name and value are the same, you can just say <code>Cache { i_cache, … }</code>.)</p>
<p>Yours may be a bit big but you can of course use other expressions which evaluate to a field value:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>Foo {
    bar: get_bar(),
    baz: Baz {
        x: true,
    },
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that unlike a C++ class, there's no pre-existing <code>self</code> to write into. <code>new</code> is a normal function which produces a struct by aggregate initialization and then returns it.</p>
